This method could be written as
```c#
private static byte[] AppendEndOfLine(ReadOnlySpan<byte> span, bool lineFeedOnly)
{
    Debug.Assert(!span.IsEmpty);

    var array = new byte[span.Length + (lineFeedOnly ? 1 : 2)];

    Debug.Assert(array.Length >= 2);

    array[^2] = ByteCR;
    array[^1] = ByteLF;

    span.CopyTo(array);
    return array;
}
```
So just always write the CR/LF sequence, then in CopyTo it gets overwritten.
This saves the branch from `if (!lineFeedOnly)`. Note: I expecte PGO will emit similar code (+ the fallback code-path if the assumption isn't met), but this saves total IL and machine code.

---

<details>
  <summary>going "Unsafe"</summary>

One could also go "Unsafe" to remove the bound-checks from `array[^2]` (and `^1`):
```c#
private static byte[] AppendEndOfLine(ReadOnlySpan<byte> span, bool lineFeedOnly)
{
    Debug.Assert(!span.IsEmpty);

    var array = new byte[span.Length + (lineFeedOnly ? 1 : 2)];

    Debug.Assert(array.Length >= 2);

    ref byte ra = ref MemoryMarshal.GetArrayDataReference(array);
    ra = ref Unsafe.Add(ref ra, (uint)(array.Length - 2));
    Unsafe.As<byte, short>(ref ra) = BitConverter.IsLittleEndian
        ? (short)(ByteCR | ByteLF << 8)
        : (short)(ByteLF | ByteCR << 8);

    span.CopyTo(array);
    return array;
}
```
</details>

In a micro-benchmark it looks like:
```
